6E-DIFF-REVIEW: APPROVE-WITH-NITS

Non-author diff review of slice 6e on branch `srvm-6e` (base `73c28036`), read against plan v2.1 (`1792145566000-architect-6d-plan-v21.report.md` §§3.5/3.6/3.9-6e), the binding architect line for item 6 (`1792161400000-architect-6da-nit1-glm.glm-5-3.findings.md`), the takeover report (`1792210147000-engineer-6e-takeover.report.md`), and the full diff (`1792225000000-6e-full.diff`, all 1,767 lines), with the applied tree at `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-6e` spot-read at every site cited below. No P0 or P1 found. The two-author interplay is clean: opus's four fixes to glm's partial are each real, correctly scoped, and pinned; glm's core machinery (lift/lower-running, dead-prefix, oracle traversal body) survived takeover intact and reads correctly.

## Findings by priority

**No P0, no P1, no P2 in the diff itself.** The P2-class items in this review are pre-landing gates the report itself declares unrun (cljd compile, cljstyle, non-JVM lanes) — listed under "gate and landing" below, not as diff defects.

**P3-1 — The hand-built repeated-register pin exercises the lift only; the lower half of the clause is unpinned.** `test/yin/vm/semantic/layout_test.cljc` (`a-repeated-operand-agrees-and-a-divergence-refuses`, diff lines 851–891) builds the hand-written layout (`:layouts {3 [2]}` over the well-formed `repeated-operand-base`), calls `layout/lift-running` on a hand-assembled kernel state, and asserts window agreement plus the divergence refusal naming register 2. It never calls `lower-running`/`kernel-task` on the agreed window, so the reconstruction half — `region-slice` (`layout.cljc:789-796`) re-emitting register 2 at both sequence positions from one window entry, and the byte-equal relift — is asserted nowhere for a repeated register. This matters because the canonical window collapses the repeat to one entry by construction, so lower is the only place a position could be dropped or reordered. Minimal fix: in the same deftest, lower the agreeing payload and assert the receiver region equals `[42 f 42 7]` (the source stack) with a byte-equal relift; one assertion block, no new machinery. This is a nit on the flagged deviation (see opinion 1), not a reason to reopen the design.

**P3-2 — `region-window` canonicalizes dead-ambient sequence values before filtering them out.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/layout.cljc:636-641`: `values` maps `canonical-value` over the whole `layout(p) ++ uses(p)` sequence, and only afterwards `(keep #(when (contains? live …) %))` drops entries outside `L(p)`. A dead-ambient layout entry (the §3.5-amended static domain: operand PCs of an abandoned enclosing application, where layout entries sit outside `L(p)`) holding a value the canonicalizer refuses (e.g. a closure whose image is absent from the space) would make the stack lift refuse at a boundary where the semantic lane — whose run window is `L(pc)` only and never walks that slot — lifts fine: a law-4 disagreement manufactured by the lift, in the direction of over-refusal. I could not construct this from a real program (a dead ambient slot holds the program's own operator/operand values, whose images are in the space, and the corpus's dead-ambient cases pass), so this is a latent asymmetry, not an observed failure. Minimal fix if the gate wants it closed: canonicalize only positions whose register is in `live`, carrying positional placeholders for the rest so `take-top` count alignment and the repeated-register agreement check (which only needs live positions) are unchanged — or explicitly document in §4.3 that stack-lane dead-ambient values must be canonicalizable. Either is acceptable; silently leaving it is also defensible given unreachability, but the choice should be conscious.

**P3-3 — Harness robustness: `boundary-site` lifts outside the refusal-catching path.** `boundary-record` (harness, diff lines 997–1013) computes `(boundary-site m)` before the `try` that turns a `lane-lift` refusal into a `:refusal` record; `boundary-site` on a kernel calls `layout/lift-running` directly (diff lines 977–982). A kernel boundary where `lift-running` itself refuses (unknown image, bad stack bases — hand-built or future-corpus cases) would throw out of the driver as a test error rather than recording a refusal all three lanes could be compared on. In the current corpus this cannot bite (leading refusals are rows discharge refusals; `lift-running` succeeds at every measured boundary, as the green 99k-assertion slow run shows), so it is a nit — but the next corpus addition that does bite will present as a harness crash, not a finding. Minimal fix: wrap the `boundary-site` computation in the same `try` and record `{:tag :refusal … :site nil}` (alignment then fails loudly on sequence mismatch, which is the intended signal).

**Checked and found correct (not defects) — the areas this review was asked to stress:**

- *Relabel scoping (opus fix 1).* `h->a` (`layout.cljc`, diff lines 230–246) relabels a `:push` only when the new `run?` argument is true, and the only `true` call site is `lift-running` (`layout.cljc:661` in the applied tree); `stack-site` (delivery states, closure entries) goes through the strict arity and refuses. The `layout-refusals` clause "a delivery entry whose parking instruction would be a :push refuses… naming the push pc" (diff lines 1737–1750) pins exactly the invented-state failure mode, and the report's mutant table shows glm's relabel-everywhere version is killed by it. The successor-existence and non-adjacency reasoning is sound.
- *Oracle direction (opus fix 2).* `oracle-refusal` (diff lines 1180–1218) is genuinely per-receiver: semantic → nil, stack → `:retained-ffi` only, register → `:retained-ffi` or `:continuation-registers` via the full-payload traversal (run rows slot 17, waits slot 13, ready state/value/refusal/completion-`:V` fields slot 18, parked slot 8 — matching `lower-defect` at applied `layout.cljc:1180-1195`, including the completion-descriptor alignment via `[:D] ++ completion-table[tag]`, which I verified against `rows.cljc:49-56`). The stack-over-refusal mutant (92 failures) confirms the old direction-blind oracle would have passed a wrong stack refusal and the new one does not.
- *Alignment machinery.* `kernel-tick`'s step-past-the-push (diff lines 931–941) executes only the `:push` itself (pc advance per `stack.cljc`), not the successor A instruction; `lane-root-records`' pending-stutter comparison (lines 1036–1047) pairs each stutter with the next observed boundary and asserts none is left pending; `alignment-sequence` compares `[A pc]` sites, so cross-lane alignment is on canonical pcs, not raw kernel pcs. Law 4's refusal handling requires refusals to be identical across lanes with zero serves and to form a contiguous leading run (lines 1250–1254) — the Q6 discharge false-refusal shape, not a per-boundary escape hatch.
- *Dead prefix.* `slice-window`'s `≥` with `take-top` (applied `layout.cljc:564-574`), `region-window`'s excess accounting (`:623-641`), `dead-prefix!` recording into diagnostics only, `region-slice` producing no prefix on lower (`:789-796`), and `stack-k`'s cumulative bases are mutually consistent; the layout_test cases pin diagnostics exactly (`[{1 @11} {1 @12}]`, the six-entry frame-slice list), pin "lower produces no prefix" by receiver region count (which law 2 cannot see — opus was right to add it), and pin that a dead-prefix value is never walked (foreign closure dropped vs the same closure in the live region refusing `:unknown-image`). Lower-side nil fill for dead-ambient entries (`region-slice`, applied `:789-796`) matches the §4.3 amendment text in the diff.
- *Run-mode guards.* `safepoint!` run mode (applied `layout.cljc:345-349`) accepts exactly `0 ≤ pc < count`; `virt*` with the explicit set refuses ambiguity within `L(p)` and reads absent slots as absent (no named fallback) — the register injectivity check of plan §3.5 survives as a refusal rather than a silent pick. `canonical-task`/`kernel-task` ready handling (diff lines 589–596, 644–651) mirrors the semantic ready reconstruction, and `kernel-task` correctly forces `:halted?/:blocked?` false and `:value nil` when running.

**On (c), weakened assertions / pass-by-construction — one structure deserves explicit gate attention, and survives it.** In `aligned-case` (diff lines 1392–1410), the `refused` map is *extracted from law 5's own outcomes* (preferring the `[:semantic to]` direction) and then `law-5-pin` is compared against law 5 — for the extraction-source direction that comparison is partly tautological. It is not pass-by-construction overall, for three independent reasons: (i) each individual transfer/refusal is asserted against `oracle-refusal` per boundary in `aligned-transfer-observer` (lines 1283–1305), so kinds and attach counts are judged independently of the tallies; (ii) the equality then genuinely tests source-independence — a boundary refusing from `:stack` but transferring from `:semantic` breaks it; (iii) the extracted `refused` map is itself compared against the hard-coded `aligned-quick-pins`/`aligned-slow-pins` in `check-aligned-cases`, so absolute counts and kinds per case are pinned externally (and the pins are non-trivial: e.g. row 1 bare 6 register refusals, row 4 exactly 2 retained-FFI per backend). The mutant campaign (oracle skips run rows: 93 failures; register under-refusal: 93) corroborates. No change requested; the gate should simply understand the pin tables, not the law-5 comparison alone, are the load-bearing counts.

## Independent opinion on the two flagged items

**Flag 1 — the hand-built-vector deviation: ACCEPT; the architect should amend the binding line, not send the slice back.** The binding line's premise is factually wrong, and the implementation proves it rather than asserting it: `code/well-formed?` on the literal `[:call 0 1 [2 2]]` vector returns `{:rule :not-expression-structured, :pc 2}`, pinned as the first assertion of the deftest. The acting-architect's ruling read `code.cljc`'s item-10 operand rule ("registers only") in isolation; the full grammar also requires expression structure, and a parse tree cannot give one expression's result two operand positions — the same reason the linearizer never repeats a register in projected code. So "the executed ordered-region comparison for such a vector" names an execution that cannot exist in A, and no test seam inside the corpus can manufacture it. What the clause can mean in reality — two region positions of one register decoding to agreeing values, divergence refusing — is exercised at exactly its only reachable site, `region-window`, via the hand-built layout, and the genuinely executed form of operand multiplicity (one value, two distinct operand registers, region holding `[7 7]`, one root on three lanes) is pinned by `ordered-operand-multiplicity` and the `:repeated-operand` hand-built aligned case under laws 4 and 5. My one addition is P3-1 (pin the lower reconstruction for the repeated register) — with that, coverage of the clause is complete for every shape that can occur. The gate should record the literal line as superseded-by-measurement and amend its wording for future slices, in the same spirit as the ruling's own retreat on its nit-2 premise.

**Flag 2 — row 4 lowered into the backends before its request is retained: WITHIN the fragment; confirm.** The backend fragment has never been a list of row labels; it is defined by `lower-defect`'s payload judgement (image collision, installs, free-env, retained-FFI, register primitives), and plan v2.1 §3.9-6e retains "the backend fragment and existing obstruction list unchanged" — i.e. the judgement, not a case whitelist. Before retention, row 4's task contains no `:ffi-request` pending in slot 13, so there is nothing a de Bruijn kernel cannot hold, and refusing those boundaries would be a refusal invented from case identity rather than state — the same category error as the original ready-queue refusal. The pins confirm the boundary is drawn at the state, not the row: row 4 bare measures 36 boundaries with exactly 2 `:retained-ffi` refusals into each backend (the post-retention ones) and transfers elsewhere, and the de Bruijn lanes already ran row 4 as sources under the pre-6e laws. Confirm opus's reading at the gate.

## What the gate and landing lanes should watch

1. **The report's own BLOCKED gate is real and unrun**: local cljd compile of the listed namespaces and `cljstyle check` on the four files (takeover report §Lanes/§Ending). Note the brief to me said the diff "includes the cljstyle reformat" while the report says cljstyle was NOT RUN — landing should run `cljstyle check` and treat any reformat as a separate mechanical commit, not fold it silently into the feat commit. The new multi-arity shapes (`virt`, `safepoint!`, `h->a` — none holding destructuring `fn`s, which was the actual 6d-a NPE pattern) make the cljd compile a genuine check, not a formality.
2. **Mutant campaign freshness**: the nine mutants were run before the final single-arity refactor and stutter-pin edit, on the report's claim of behaviour-preservation. Focused/harness/quick reruns cover behaviour, but if the gate wants mutant evidence on the exact landing tree, re-run the campaign; at minimum re-run the two mutants touching edited code (relabel-everywhere, oracle direction).
3. **Cross-host pins are JVM-measured**: Node and Dart quick tiers, and the three-host slow tier, are the orchestrator's. Per the report's own rule, a Node/Dart difference in boundary/relabel/refusal counts is a cross-host finding, not a remint — landing must not "fix" a red Node lane by regenerating pins from Node output. Conversely, if all three hosts reproduce the JVM pins exactly, that is strong evidence and should be recorded as such.
4. **The baseline discrepancy** (report §Acceptance: brief says 6d-a baseline 4,090 tests, this tree implies 4,079 + 7 new = 4,086): reconcile which number is master truth before landing, or the landing test-count gate is meaningless in both directions.
5. **Ready-root oracle coverage**: the aligned corpus's register refusals should include at least one boundary whose oracle prim path runs through `[:ready …]` (slot 18 state/value/completion), exercising plan §3.6's "only unsupported primitive nested in a queued outcome" case on the backend lanes, not only via 6d-a's semantic-lane item 13. If no aligned boundary's refusal path is ready-rooted, add one targeted case; the traversal code in both `lower-defect` and the oracle is written for it and currently may only be mutant-covered, not corpus-covered.
6. **Commit hygiene**: the proposed three-commit mapping (layout.cljc / tests / docs) is right; ensure `collab/*`, `target/*`, root `target-6e-*`, and glm's relocated probe never enter any commit, and that the §8.8 docs text lands in the same series so the design's "Implemented" claim never precedes the code on master.

Net: the slice implements plan v2.1 §3.5/§3.6/§3.9-6e faithfully — running lift/lower on both backends, run-only `:push` relabel, dead-prefix drop/record/never-in-frame, per-direction oracle with exact pins, laws 4/5 at every aligned boundary of all 61 cases plus the hand-built programs — with both flagged deviations correctly resolved by the implementers. APPROVE-WITH-NITS on P3-1 (lower-side repeated-register pin), P3-2 (dead-ambient canonicalization asymmetry, fix or consciously document), and P3-3 (harness refusal recording), none of which should block the architectural gate; the gate's substantive work is confirming the two flagged readings and enforcing the unrun landing lanes above.
